1040: quantity, is assigned to a source-family return.
1041: 
1042: ## IF.12. Full old-v / new-epsilon annular family comparison
1043: 
1044: Fix an integer root-scalar winding \(j\), and use
1045: \[
1046:  r(\epsilon)=\epsilon^j u(\epsilon)r_0,\qquad
1047:  \lambda=r^m ,
1048: \]
1049: where the specified nonzero unit \(u\) has an extending
1050: logarithm. The comparison in this section is over the
1051: punctured epsilon annulus carrying this actual path.
1052: It does not assume a trivial Isom torsor over a product
1053: of two independent punctured parameter discs.
1054: 
1055: The source map (IF.1), the canonical divisor-line map,
1056: and (IF.6)--(IF.7) identify the full ordinary suspension.
1057: The selected Tate \(Q,P\) agree under this source
1058: isomorphism. Their physical heights differ by
1059: \(q_{\rm old}=\epsilon^{-4}q_*\), an actual single-valued
1060: integer-power unit on this annulus. Its powers act on
1061: the toric character charts and preserve the chosen
1062: periodic fan and physical delta. Its winding is retained.
1063: 
1064: On each simply connected parameter chart use the actual
1065: root map (IF.8)--(IF.9). It carries the marked source
1066: section, the resolution ideals and the corrected Cartier
1067: line. The finite source/line map has the form \(A=LD\),
1068: where \(L\) covers \(-nR\). Its central commutator
1069: character is one: in the good branch \(R=O\) centrally
1070: and \(L\) is a scalar; in the reduced branch the
1071: corrected root line is trivial on the compact reduced
1072: III fibre, so the multipliers of \(D,L\) are constants,
1073: and their source maps commute. The finite averaging
1074: conjugation of the actual norm-one unit gives
1075: \[
1076:  DLD^{-1}=L,\qquad A^4D^{-4}=L^4 .
1077: \]
1078: The source part of \(L^4\) specializes to the identity
1079: in both branches. Divide its central scalar and take
1080: its small extending Lie logarithm. The actual annular
1081: difference from four times the logarithm of \(L\)
1082: retains the period (IF.10), with its full line factor
1083: and signed \(n\). No multiple of that period is
1084: asserted to be an extending reduced-root logarithm.
1085: 
1086: Choose one reference \(\epsilon_*\) and transport the
1087: entire old ordinary-to-cap overlap through these
1088: source/root/Cartier arrows, including \(d\delta\),
1089: the chosen root cut, norm and finite character.
1090: Use this full map as the reference of the new
1091: \(F_0\). On a fixed new cap annulus the source and
1092: root families are smooth through epsilon zero.
1093: Restriction to a chosen root sheet identifies
1094: the ordinary line quotient with the cap quotient:
1095: an element \(A^hB^\ell\) fixing that sheet has
1096: \(h=mk\), so is exactly \(B^{nk+\ell}\).
1097: Thus the local comparisons are literal source
1098: and line maps, and their sheet changes are the
1099: retained powers of \(A\).
1100: 
1101: Correct those maps at \(\epsilon_*\) to the full
1102: reference overlap. On two angular base charts
1103: their overlap translations have Lie logarithms
1104: zero at that reference. Lift along the simply
1105: connected epsilon disc. Every ordinary and
1106: finite-group discrepancy is an integral period
1107: and is zero at the reference, hence stays zero.
1108: The fixed-contour Cauchy splitting in the base
1109: variable therefore glues the actual local maps
1110: to \(F_0\), holomorphically in epsilon. This
1111: retains its full annular integer and the
1112: doubled reduced period. It is the explicit
1113: Hom/source-period torsor construction of
1114: RCZ.5, with the present displayed root maps
1115: as its input.
1116: 
1117: Now compare the two completed families on a
1118: small simply connected parameter chart.
1119: Their total ordinary/root/cusp lattice
1120: two-cocycles agree under the actual source
1121: period identification: they agree at the
1122: reference, and their integral discrepancies
1123: are continuous in the parameter. The reduced
1124: component translations are first removed by
1125: their actual suspension powers. The remaining
1126: Lie bundle has the literal coarse-base exact
1127: sequence
1128: \[
1129:      0\longrightarrow\mathcal O\delta
1130:        \longrightarrow V\longrightarrow\mathcal O(-1)
1131:        \longrightarrow0 .
1132: \]
1133: The source dualizing vector and physical delta
1134: give its relative frames. Split the extension
1135: and its one-cocycles by the fixed-circle
1136: Cauchy/Laurent operators for \(\mathcal O(1)\),
1137: \(\mathcal O\), and \(\mathcal O(-1)\).
1138: Finite averaging preserves the full root
1139: cocycles. Exponentiation then gives actual
1140: local family isomorphisms extending on the
1141: chosen cusp and root charts.
1142: 
1143: On two parameter charts their ratio is a
1144: global extending translation. The actual
1145: source/rational-divisor endpoint argument
1146: of RCZ.4 identifies that group with the
1147: physical \(\mathcal O^*\), including all
1148: finite-kernel choices. To glue the local
1149: maps, take logarithms \(\ell\) of their
1150: scalar ratio on the two angular overlap
1151: components, choose \(\chi_++\chi_-=1\),
1152: and set \(b_+=\chi_-\ell,b_-=-\chi_+\ell\).
1153: Their common \(\bar\partial\) is solved on
1154: a smaller annulus by
1155: \[
1156:  v(\epsilon)=\frac1\pi\int_{\mathbb C}
1157:        \frac{\widetilde f(w)}{\epsilon-w}\,dA(w).
1158: \]
1159: Then \(h_\pm=\exp(b_\pm-v)\) have ratio
1160: equal to the actual scalar transition,
1161: and \(h_\pm^{-1}I_\pm\) glue literally.
1162: The cutoff is supported in the larger
1163: annulus and equals one on the loop.
1164: This construction retains both overlap
1165: components and any integral scalar winding.
1166: 
1167: There is a concrete forward-endpoint
1168: factor before this last scalar splitting.
1169: For the root winding \(j\), the old and
1170: new ordinary forward decks have ratio
1171: \[
1172:       q_{\rm old}^{nj}/q_*^{nj}
1173:                          =\epsilon^{-4nj}.
1174: \]
1175: With \(\tau=\log\epsilon/(2\pi i)\), retain
1176: the actual compensating cochain
1177: \[
1178:  c_0(\tau)=\exp\{2\pi i\,2nj\,\tau(\tau-1)\},
